[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Generate the grammar from a typed Nix option surface, and gate it

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[SLICE]
UID: SLICE-GRAMMAR-FROM-NIX
TITLE: Generate the grammar from a typed Nix option surface, and gate it
DEPTH: verified
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Generate a typed Nix option surface from strictdoc's own grammar definition,
and generate docs/sdoc/grammar.sgra from that surface. The surface is derived,
not hand-written: nothing about what a grammar may contain is typed by a
person.

Two layers. The first is faithful -- it expresses exactly what a .sgra file can
express, with no opinion of ours in it. The second is normalized: named
converters turn faithful shapes into better Nix types, so a regex that is a
pure literal alternation becomes an enum, a two-value alternation becomes a
boolean, an optional group becomes a nullable with a null default. A shape no
converter recognises is an error rather than a fallback to free text, so an
upstream grammar change fails during the update sweep instead of reaching
anyone. Declaring a value unconstrained is itself a converter, keeping "we
decided this is free text" distinguishable from "we did not recognise this".

The extractor runs at generation time, outside Nix, and emits Nix source that
is committed; Nix never parses strictdoc during evaluation. A drift check
regenerates and diffs, so a stale surface is a red check.

How the conversion is expressed was open when this was written. It is not now:
the SECOND shape shipped, and both layers are committed files. What follows
records both shapes because the losing argument is what makes the choice cheap
to revisit.

Converters can live inside the extractor, working on the parsed grammar. That
preserves why each shape was classified as it was: a pattern is known to have
come from a particular grammar rule rather than inferred from how it rendered.

Or the extractor emits the faithful surface and a declarative rewrite
transforms it. Measured: ast-grep is tree-sitter based and ships a Nix grammar,
so it parses our shapes with nothing added; a rule of about fifteen lines turns
a literal-alternation pattern into an enum; and the fail-closed condition is
one more catch-all rule at error severity rather than a special case in code.
Both the tool and its Python bindings are packaged already, and the bindings
work on Nix source in process, so this needs no second toolchain and no
separate file format -- the rewrite can live in the same script as the
extraction.

The remaining cost of the second shape is provenance, and it is the whole
argument. It matches generated text rather than the grammar behind it, so a
two-value alternation that means a boolean is indistinguishable from one that
merely looks like it.

TAKEN, and the cost accepted: extract/normalize.py parses the emitted faithful
Nix source with ast_grep_py, and lib/faithful.nix is committed. The provenance
loss is mitigated rather than avoided -- decompose.py calls back into
normalize.py so both classify the same nodes, which keeps the two halves from
drifting apart even though neither can see the grammar rule any more.

Our own grammar values -- the five node types with their fields and relations
-- are data filling the generated surface, not part of it.

Add the validation gate: a clean-directory strictdoc export over the whole
corpus at commit time and in CI. This is not a second opinion. None of
strictdoc's grammar checks run on a bare parse, so the corpus export is the
only validation that ever happens to a generated grammar.

What belongs at evaluation is only what strictdoc has no rule for anywhere: two
fields with the same title, and two relations with the same type and role.
Both are ASSERTED against rather than made unrepresentable. Keying on name was
the intent here and it did not survive implementation: elements, fields and
relations are all lists, never attribute sets, because Nix sorts attribute-set
keys and strictdoc enforces order. So sgra.nix throws on duplicate titles,
duplicate relations and duplicate element tags, each message naming which one
strictdoc would silently have kept.
Everything strictdoc does enforce is left to strictdoc and becomes a negative
fixture proving the gate fires.

Consumer surface the operator reviews before implementation: the normalized
option tree and the converter list. Reviewed after: the regenerated grammar as
an empty diff, the gate firing on a deliberately broken fixture, and an
unrecognised grammar shape failing the extractor.

Not included: behaviour, transitions, ownership rules, which fields count as
contract, which node types a relation may point at, and any ordering policy of
ours. Those are the semantic layer, defined by a consumer of this surface
rather than by it.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Generating rather than hand-writing is the operator's call, and the reason is
drift: the overlay now tracks a project shipping roughly three releases a
month, and a hand-written surface does not notice a knob being added. It keeps
generating a valid grammar that cannot express the new thing, with nothing
going red.

Measured, and it is derivable: strictdoc assembles its own grammar into a
string that is byte-identical across the last two releases; a metamodel built
from it reaches 25 of 43 rules from the root, and its abstract rules yield the
four field types and three relation types without a list being typed by hand --
including that file relations have no reverse role at all. The metamodel alone
is not sufficient, reporting every attribute as mandatory and flattening value
vocabularies, so optionality and literal sets come from the parser tree beneath
it. Both are reachable from the same object.

Also measured, and the reason two checks stay at evaluation: a grammar
declaring one field twice and one relation twice exports clean with exit zero,
and the duplicates resolve in opposite directions -- fields keep the last
declaration, relations keep the first. No rule against either exists anywhere
in strictdoc.

Carried from the spike: a typed surface regenerates the grammar byte-for-byte
in 0.03 s and rejects thirteen of thirteen bad declarations once the merged
value is forced; strictdoc rejects six of six injected grammar defects; the
gate needs nothing but the strictdoc package; and cross-field checks must live
in one place that forces evaluation of the whole value, because Nix's addCheck
never runs on a nested option.
<<<
PARENT_FP: >>>
DEC-EXECUTABLE-RULES-ROADMAP:0000000
DEC-FP-FIELDS-CONFIGURABLE:0000000
<<<
NOTES: >>>
The faithful layer carries every native grammar feature by construction, so
nothing needs listing as a deliberate inclusion. What the NORMALIZED layer
exposes is a scoping decision and is open below.

What the implementation settled, each of which had been left open here:

Element order is carried by the VALUES. Elements, fields and relations are all
lists, never attribute sets, because Nix sorts attribute-set keys and strictdoc
enforces order -- so the conceptual order survives and no reshuffle was needed.
The consequence is that duplicate detection is an explicit assertion rather than
a structural side effect, which is why the two duplicate checks exist at all.

Both layers are materialised as committed files, and the component is general
purpose: the normalized layer carries every native grammar feature, including
the composite nodes, view styles, human titles, child relations and file roles
this repository never uses. Those are exercised by
packages/strictdoc-grammar/fixtures/foreign.nix, which round-trips
byte-identically, and the five node types are one consumer in
packages/strictdoc-grammar/values.nix rather than part of the surface.

Two corrections the extraction needed, both found by running the chain:

A '^'-anchored negative lookahead is POSITIONAL, not a value constraint.
FieldName is /(?!^UID)(?!^RELATIONS).../ and arpeggio matches with re.MULTILINE
at the current position, so the lookahead can only fire at a line start -- which
a field title, sitting after the literal '  - TITLE: ', never is. Hoisting those
two into a value-level denial produced a type that rejected a field named UID,
which this repository's own grammar declares five times.

That was true of both when written and is now true of only one. bb14e09c split
them: RELATIONS is an alternative of the rule that denies it, so it can never be
a legal field name and IS enforced as a value-level denial (deny). UID is not,
so it stays recorded under denyAtLineStart and unenforced. Measured through the
package's own entry points: a field titled RELATIONS is rejected by the types
and accepted by emit.grammar alone, which proves the rejection is the surface's
and not the emitter's; a field titled UID is accepted by both.

The choice vocabulary could not keep its upstream name. textx calls it options,
and a submodule whose CONFIG carries a key named options is read by the Nix
module system as an option DECLARATION, so every sibling key errors out. The
extractor now renames reserved module-system attribute names, and a collision
with no rename is fatal rather than silent.

WHAT CLOSED THIS SLICE. The operator ruled on 2026-08-26 that three items stood
between implemented and accepted. All three landed 2026-08-27, and each carries
its own record of what was built rather than restating it here:

  MECH-STRICTDOC-DEVENV-MODULE -- DONE. ai.strictdoc, with enable, package and
    grammars, in packages/strictdoc-grammar/modules/devenv/. Its
    grammars.<name>.elements is declared with the NORMALIZED type, so the
    module is a seam values cannot cross unchecked.
  MECH-GRAMMAR-EXTRACT-SOURCE-BOUNDARY -- DONE, and by neither route that node
    proposed. The overlay was DELETED rather than thinned, because the runner
    converts no upstream package and so had nothing left for an overlay to own.
    packages/strictdoc-grammar/lib/mkExtract.nix is the only copy; flake.nix
    builds it once for the checks and the module reuses that derivation.
  MECH-STRICTDOC-UPSTREAM-FLAKE -- DONE. overlays/dev-tools/strictdoc.nix
    re-exports inputs.strictdoc.packages.<system>.default by plain attrset
    update. Both dependency adjustments died with the first-party build.

The fourth thing settled at the same time is also DONE. docs/sdoc/grammar.sgra
is now WRITTEN, by the module's own generate:sgra task from
ai.strictdoc.grammars.repo in devenv.nix. The bytes it writes are identical to
the ones that were already committed, so the one deliberate normalization commit
acceptance item 5 allows for was not spent.

WHAT IS STILL OPEN. The three questions this node recorded as needing a round
with the operator did not move together:

  Whether the grammar gates belong in the flake checks output -- UNCHANGED and
    still the operator's. The five are registered there, which is what makes
    them addressable by name and, inseparably, what puts them in CI
    (MECH-GRAMMAR-CHECKS-ARE-IN-FLAKE-CHECK). The devenv task still restates
    five of the six acceptance arms instead of deferring to them
    (MECH-GRAMMAR-GATES-ARE-WRITTEN-TWICE), which is held rather than fixed
    because the boundary it needs is not written down
    (MECH-CHECK-SYSTEM-BOUNDARY).
  Whether the emitter stays reachable without the type in front of it -- PARTLY
    answered. This repository's own path can no longer bypass the surface,
    because the module declares its input with the normalized type, and the
    emitter split into a renderer plus denormalize as designed
    (MECH-EMIT-LAYER-BOUNDARY). What is still true is that a consumer importing
    the library can call emit.grammar directly and pass through no type at all
    (MECH-DSL-CHECKS-NOTHING-BY-ITSELF), and whether the inner renderer is
    named or published at all is undecided.
  Whether the line-anchored denials are enforced anywhere -- ANSWERED and built.
    Each denied word is now run against the sibling alternatives of the rule
    that denies it: RELATIONS is an alternative of nothing and is enforced, UID
    is an alternative of SDocNodeField and is not. What remains is that
    FieldName's two answers come from a register rather than from the document
    grammar, which is what MECH-LINE-ANCHORED-DENIAL-UNENFORCED is now about.

Unchanged and still leaving this slice: contract-bearing fields are semantic --
see MECH-CONTRACT-FIELDS-EDGE-TO-SEMANTIC-LAYER; the PARENT_FP entry moves with
that edge and only the operator changes those. HTML rendering is a requirement
of its own rather than a property of this slice, tracked as
MECH-HTML-RENDER-MUST-PASS.

Three costs the closure round created, logged rather than fixed:
MECH-STRICTDOC-VERSION-SMOKE, because dropping the first-party build dropped
the installCheckPhase that asserted the binary's version;
MECH-STRICTDOC-DEVENV-MODULE-NOT-PUBLISHED, because options-doc demands exact
home-manager parity and so a devenv-only ai.* facet cannot be exported, leaving
ai.strictdoc importable only by this repository; and SLICE-STRICTDOC-OVERLAY's
STATEMENT, which specified a build that no longer existed. The third is closed:
that statement was rewritten in place on 2026-08-27, supersession not being
expressible for a slice (MECH-SLICE-CANNOT-BE-SUPERSEDED).

DEPTH WAS HELD at implemented until an independent read happened.
verified means an INDEPENDENT session checked the code against this node, and
for a while none had: every paragraph above was written by a session in the same
run as the work it describes, and the run that assembled this section built nothing and ran
no check. This slice's five flake gates are strictdoc-grammar-model-equal (which
is two gates, semantic equality AND byte identity, and its own header says so),
-foreign-roundtrip, -negative-fixtures, -surface-current and -surface-live, plus
the module-eval arm for the option path. strictdoc-grammar-corpus is registered
beside them but belongs to SLICE-SDOC-IN-SEMBLE and the patched tree-sitter
grammar, not here. All are real mechanical evidence, but a green check proves
the code does what the code does, not that it does what this node says.

VERIFIED 2026-08-27 by an independent session that wrote none of this code, and
DEPTH is raised on the strength of it. Re-measured rather than transcribed: the
grammar renders byte-identically at 4085 bytes, with a positive control -- one
PREFIX mutated in a scratch copy produces a diff, so the comparison is live. The
denial split holds in all four arms with a passing control title. The
fail-closed path was reproduced by hand at exit 2 against a control at exit 0.
All five gates plus the module arms build green at bc646f12.

Five divergences were found and are fixed above: the anchored-denial paragraph,
the conversion shape still framed as open, the duplicate-detection claim, this
evidence list, and a MECH-CHECK-SYSTEM-BOUNDARY reference wrapped across a line
break so that it resolved to nothing. Four further findings were NOT this
node's to fix and are logged as their own nodes instead: the fail-closed arm has
no standing gate, the devenv module builds a second copy of the extraction
derivation rather than reusing the flake's, wrapped UIDs in prose are a class
rather than a typo, and the groomed nodes were left in the backlog file.

One promise in the governing decision is still unmet and is tracked separately:
DEC-EXECUTABLE-RULES-ROADMAP says milestone one delivers a validation gate at
commit time AND in CI. The CI half exists. devenv.nix declares no strictdoc
hook, so the commit-time half does not.
